# HalfLifeBench PoC

A Python CLI benchmark measuring how SOC policy directive adherence decays as
conversational context grows. Model-agnostic, currently targeting GPT-4.1-nano
(model-under-test) and Claude Sonnet 4.5 (judge).

## Key Concepts

- **Directives D1-D10**: ten SOC policies injected via system/developer prompt.
- **Model defaults**: `OPENAI_MODEL=gpt-4.1-nano`, `ANTHROPIC_MODEL=claude-sonnet-4-5`.
- **depth_tokens_measured**: authoritative metric derived from the API's
  `request_input_tokens_total`, not tiktoken. tiktoken is planning-only.
- **Half-life**: depth where pass rate drops below 50% of the depth-0 baseline (B).
  Not the logistic midpoint.
- **Current run profile**: baselines + sweep only (near-probe refresh removed).

## Project Layout

```
halflifebench/         # Core library
  config.py            # Depth targets, model IDs, seed
  providers/           # Model abstraction (OpenAI + Anthropic)
  filler.py            # Filler generation + validation
  runner.py            # Baselines and sweep
  judge.py             # CoT-aware parsing, rule-based pre-check, spot-check
  scorer.py            # Pass rates, half-life, logistic fit
  report.py            # Self-contained HTML report generator
data/                  # Static assets (probes, directives, judge prompts, golden set)
results/               # Generated output (gitignored)
run.py                 # CLI entry point
```

## Conventions

- Python 3.11+. Type hints everywhere. No classes where a function suffices.
- API keys in `.env` (never committed). Use `python-dotenv`.
- All analysis uses `depth_tokens_measured` (API-derived), never tiktoken estimates.
- Judge verdicts are PASS/FAIL JSON with CoT. Parse the last JSON block from output.
- Default to FAIL on any ambiguity (judge parse failure, low confidence, etc.).
- Filler validation must cover all 10 directives (keyword blacklist), not just secrets.

## Reference

`ARCHITECTURE.md` is the primary architecture document. It covers the research
question, conceptual model, pipeline stages, module dependency graph, key
design decisions, data file inventory, and provider abstraction -- all at
multiple levels of detail with mermaid diagrams.

`deep-research-report.md` contains the full research design (10 directives,
100 probes, 3 experiments, cost models). The report is useful context but does
not always match the current implementation exactly -- always prefer the plan
and code when they diverge.

